// WHY: the defer emitters must PROPAGATE an emit failure, not swallow it
// (t-dy1j). This is the guard for that, and it is a guard that can fail —
// see the mutation record at the bottom.
//
// Every emitter in `defer/codegen.av` used to call `ctx.emit(env, body)`
// and throw the `Result` away. A deferred body that could not be emitted was
// dropped on the floor: no diagnostic, no F-code, exit 0. The deferred call
// simply did not appear in the output and the program ran as though the
// `defer` had never been written.
//
// That swallow concealed a live bug for a full PR cycle (#1188): the block
// scope-exit passed the pre-block environment, so
//
//     { let f = open()
//       defer close(f) }        // `f` absent from the env used to emit this
//
// failed to emit and vanished. It survived 14 specs written specifically for
// defer scoping, five green CI gates and a byte-identical diff-test, and was
// caught by review rather than by any instrument. The reason none of those 14
// specs caught it is worth stating plainly: every one of them deferred
// `println("literal")`, which needs no environment to emit.
//
// ## The probe, and why this particular one
//
// To test propagation you need an expression that PASSES typeck and FAILS in
// codegen — otherwise the program is rejected before the defer path is ever
// reached, and the fixture proves nothing about defer. `println` on an ad-hoc
// union is exactly that: the union is well-typed, and codegen refuses it
// (`codegen/printing.av` — "cannot print union type directly"). Nothing about
// the choice is special beyond those two properties; it is simply the cheapest
// reachable codegen-only failure.
//
// ## Coverage
//
// There are two LIFO workers, and between them they serve five call paths.
// Each path is exercised separately, because they reach the worker through
// different call sites and a propagation fix applied to one is not evidence
// about the other:
//
//   emit_stack_filtered  <- emit_defers            function exit, normal
//                        <- emit_defers_on_error   function exit, error (errdefer)
//   emit_frames_above    <- defer_scope_exit       nested-block scope exit
//                        <- defer_scope_exit       block-EXPRESSION scope exit
//                        <- emit_defers_above      break / continue unwind
//
// Every failing case is paired with a positive control that is the SAME
// program with an emittable deferred body. Without the control, a fixture that
// failed to compile for some unrelated reason would still satisfy the
// diagnostic assertion and the spec would pass for the wrong reason. `break`
// and `continue` get one control each: they reach `emit_defers_above` from
// separate arms, so neither is evidence about the other.

use @std.avrac.core.{avra_shell_exec_status, avra_selfhost_read_file}
use @std.avrac.test_runner.{write_fixture}

/// What a probe compile produced: the compiler's real exit status, and its
/// diagnostics.
type CompileProbe = { status: int, out: string }

/// Compile `source` and report BOTH the exit status and the diagnostics.
///
/// The status is taken from `avra_shell_exec_status` rather than inferred from
/// a sentinel echoed by a `&&`/`||` chain. That is not only tidier — the
/// original defect was that a failed deferred body produced "no diagnostic, no
/// F-code, NO NON-ZERO EXIT", and a spec that only reads stdout cannot speak to
/// the third of those. Every case below asserts the status directly.
///
/// `slot` keeps each case on its own paths: test files run as parallel units
/// (d4jv), so shared fixture and log paths would let two cases race and read
/// each other's source or output.
fn defer_compile(slot: string, source: string) -> CompileProbe {
    let path = "/tmp/avra_defer_propagate_${slot}.av"
    let log = "/tmp/avra_defer_propagate_${slot}.log"
    write_fixture(path, source)
    let status = avra_shell_exec_status("./build/bs2 compile '${path}' > '${log}' 2>&1")
    CompileProbe { status: status, out: avra_selfhost_read_file(log) }
}

/// The union declarations every fixture shares. `pick` returns an ad-hoc union,
/// which is well-typed and unprintable — the codegen-only failure this file
/// needs.
fn preamble() -> string {
    "type A = { a: int }
type B = { b: int }
fn pick(n: int) -> A | B { if n == 0 { A { a: 1 } } else { B { b: 2 } } }
"
}

/// True when the output carries the codegen refusal, wherever it came from.
/// Asserting on the MESSAGE and not merely on `F9999` matters: F9999 is the
/// generic ICE code and plenty of unrelated failures wear it, so a bare
/// `contains("F9999")` would pass on a fixture that broke for any other reason.
fn refused(out: string) -> bool {
    out.contains("cannot print union type directly")
}

/// True when the diagnostic ALSO says which statement the failed body belonged
/// to. Propagating the error is only half of what the user needs: a deferred
/// body is emitted at the scope exit, so for a function-level `defer` the
/// underlying message describes an expression sitting nowhere near the `defer`
/// the reader has to go fix.
fn blamed(out: string, kind: string) -> bool {
    out.contains("while emitting the `${kind}` body")
}

spec "an unemittable deferred body is a hard error, not silence (t-dy1j)" {
    given "the function-exit walk (emit_defers -> emit_stack_filtered)" {
        then "a top-level `defer` whose body cannot be emitted fails the compile" {
            let p = defer_compile("fnexit", preamble() + "fn main() {
    let u = pick(0)
    defer println(u)
    println(\"body\")
}")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "defer")
        }
        then "the same shape with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            let p = defer_compile("fnexit_ok", preamble() + "fn main() {
    let u = pick(0)
    defer println(\"fine\")
    println(\"body\")
}")
            p.status == 0
        }
    }

    given "the error-return walk (emit_defers_on_error -> emit_stack_filtered)" {
        then "an `errdefer` whose body cannot be emitted fails the compile" {
            let p = defer_compile("errdefer", preamble() + "fn go(n: int) -> Result<int, string> {
    let u = pick(0)
    errdefer println(u)
    if n == 0 { return Result.Err(\"boom\") }
    Result.Ok(n)
}
fn main() { match go(0) { .Ok(v) -> println(string(v)) .Err(e) -> println(e) } }")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "errdefer")
        }
        then "the same shape with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            let p = defer_compile("errdefer_ok", preamble() + "fn go(n: int) -> Result<int, string> {
    let u = pick(0)
    errdefer println(\"fine\")
    if n == 0 { return Result.Err(\"boom\") }
    Result.Ok(n)
}
fn main() { match go(0) { .Ok(v) -> println(string(v)) .Err(e) -> println(e) } }")
            p.status == 0
        }
    }

    given "the nested-block scope exit (defer_scope_exit -> emit_frames_above)" {
        then "a block-scoped `defer` whose body cannot be emitted fails the compile" {
            // Also the exact shape of the #1188 bug: the deferred body reads a
            // binding the BLOCK declared, so it is emitted against the block's
            // own environment or not at all.
            let p = defer_compile("block", preamble() + "fn main() {
    { let u = pick(0)
      defer println(u) }
    println(\"after\")
}")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "defer")
        }
        then "the same shape with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            let p = defer_compile("block_ok", preamble() + "fn main() {
    { let u = pick(0)
      defer println(\"fine\") }
    println(\"after\")
}")
            p.status == 0
        }
    }

    given "the block-EXPRESSION scope exit (emit_block_expr -> defer_scope_exit)" {
        then "a `defer` in a block used as a value fails the compile" {
            let p = defer_compile("blockexpr", preamble() + "fn main() {
    let v = { let u = pick(0)
              defer println(u)
              7 }
    println(string(v))
}")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "defer")
        }
        then "the same shape with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            let p = defer_compile("blockexpr_ok", preamble() + "fn main() {
    let v = { let u = pick(0)
              defer println(\"fine\")
              7 }
    println(string(v))
}")
            p.status == 0
        }
    }

    given "the loop-unwind walk (emit_defers_above -> emit_frames_above)" {
        then "a `defer` unwound by `break` fails the compile" {
            let p = defer_compile("brk", preamble() + "fn main() {
    mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        let u = pick(0)
        defer println(u)
        if i == 1 { break }
        i = i + 1
    }
}")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "defer")
        }
        then "a `defer` unwound by `continue` fails the compile" {
            let p = defer_compile("cont", preamble() + "fn main() {
    mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        let u = pick(0)
        defer println(u)
        i = i + 1
        if i == 2 { continue }
    }
}")
            p.status != 0 && refused(p.out) && blamed(p.out, "defer")
        }
        then "the `continue` loop with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            // `break` and `continue` reach `emit_defers_above` from separate
            // arms, so the `break` control says nothing about this one.
            let p = defer_compile("cont_ok", preamble() + "fn main() {
    mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        let u = pick(0)
        defer println(\"fine\")
        i = i + 1
        if i == 2 { continue }
    }
}")
            p.status == 0
        }
        then "the `break` loop with an emittable body compiles (positive control)" {
            let p = defer_compile("brk_ok", preamble() + "fn main() {
    mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        let u = pick(0)
        defer println(\"fine\")
        if i == 1 { break }
        i = i + 1
    }
}")
            p.status == 0
        }
    }
}

// ## MUTATION RECORD — this guard was proven able to fail
//
// A spec asserting a compile ERROR is worth nothing until you have watched it
// go green against a compiler that should have made it red. Three mutations,
// applied one at a time with a rebuild for each, all measured against this
// file as it stands (12 cases: 6 subjects, 6 controls):
//
//   A. `emit_stack_filtered` discards its Result   10/12
//        RED: fnexit, errdefer
//
//   B. `emit_frames_above` discards its Result      8/12
//        RED: block, blockexpr, brk, cont
//
//   C. the failure still propagates, but the        6/12
//      message drops the statement kind             RED: all six subjects
//
// A and B are the two LIFO workers reverted to the pre-t-dy1j form. The SPLIT
// is the point: each reds exactly its own call paths and leaves the other
// worker's alone, so the six subjects are six independent observations rather
// than one fact asserted six times.
//
// C is what makes `blamed()` load-bearing rather than decorative. Propagation
// is untouched there — the compile still fails, `refused()` still holds — and
// six cases go red purely because the diagnostic stopped saying WHICH
// statement owned the body. So `refused()` and `blamed()` fail independently,
// and a future reword that drops the statement kind cannot pass this file.
//
// All six controls stayed green through all three mutations. That is what
// rules out the other way this spec could be worthless: a fixture failing to
// compile for an unrelated reason would satisfy the subject assertions no
// matter what the emitters do, and only the controls distinguish the two.
//
// ## What the swallow actually did, observed rather than argued
//
// Under mutation B, this program:
//
//     { let u = pick(0)
//       defer println(u)
//       println("in-block") }
//     println("after")
//
// compiles CLEAN, exits 0, and prints exactly `in-block` / `after`. The
// deferred call is not misordered or mis-emitted — it is simply GONE, and
// nothing anywhere reports that it went. That is the failure mode this guard
// exists to keep from coming back.
